368	The proper ancestors $\setb{J}$ of a class $C$ are well-formed, if
369	every pair in $\setb{J}$ satisfies one of the well-formed ancestors rules
370	shown in Figure~\ref{fig:ancestors}: $\rep{\jancestormeetrule
371	[\Delta'; \rep{J}]{J_i}{J_j}}[1 \leq i < j \leq \countofb{J}]$.
372	The well-formed ancestors rules ensure that if a class $C$ extends
373	two instances of a trait $T$, $T\obb{\alpha}$ and $T\obb{\rho}$,
374	then $C$ also extends some $T\obb{\gamma}$
375	(which may be $T\obb{\alpha}$, $T\obb{\rho}$,
376	or a third instance) that extends both $T\obb{\alpha}$ and $T\obb{\rho}$.
377	Therefore, because the rules guarantee that there always exists a single
378	minimal ancestor when a class extends multiple instances of a trait,
379	no ambiguous method calls are possible due to multiple instances of a trait
380	as discussed in Section~\ref{sec:dynamic}.
