418	The visible methods $\setb{d} = \setb{\tuple{C,M}}$ of a class 
419	obey the overloading rules if every pair in $\setb{d}$ satisfies 
420	the overloading rules described in Section~\ref{sec:rules:formal}.
421	The set $\setb{\tuple{C,M}}$ contains pairs of visible method declarations 
422	$\bar{M}$ and their defining classes $\bar{C}$.
423	
424	Rule \rncite{D-Method} checks well-formedness of method declarations.
425	For the method declaration $\mu$, it verifies that the parameter names
426	of the declaration are distinct;
427	that the class type parameters and the method type parameters
428	of the declaration are collectively distinct;
429	that types used in the declaration are well-formed;
430	that the body expression is well-formed;
431	and that the type of the body expression is a subtype of the 
432	declared return type.  In addition, as we discuss below,
433	it checks that method type parameter bindings are well-formed and
434	that any references to trait type parameters are correctly used
435	according to their variance annotations.
436	
437	A sequence of method type parameter bindings is well-formed,
438	if (1)~every reference to a method type parameter falls within its scope
439	(which extends to the right of its declaration),
440	(2)~no reference to a method type parameter appears in any of the upper bounds,
441	and (3) for each type parameter, the union of its lower bounds is a subtype
442	of the intersection of its upper bounds.
443	As we discuss in Section~\ref{sec:discuss},
444	restriction (1) makes dynamic dispatch more efficient
445	and restriction (2) makes it consistent.  Figure~\ref{fig:ancestors}
446	shows the rules for well-formed type parameter bindings.
447	
448	Trait type parameters are correctly used according to their variance annotations,
449	if covariant and contravariant type parameters appear only at
450	covariant and contravariant positions, respectively.
451	In order to express this restriction, we introduce a \textit{type context} $\T$,
452	which defines positions where a type can appear within another type.
453	Similarly to the conventional evaluation contexts~\cite{wright94},
454	a type context $\T$ is a type with one of its components replaced by a hole,
455	denoted by ``$\emptycontext$''.  The type $\T\context{\tau}$ results from
456	placing a type $\tau$ in the hole of $\T$:
457	\begin{center}
458	$\descsm{Type context} \quad 
459	\T~::=~\emptycontext ~\mid~ T\ob{\bar\tau, \T, \bar\tau} ~\mid~ O\ob{\bar\tau, \T, \bar\tau} ~\mid~ \tuple{\bar\tau, \T, \bar\tau} ~\mid~ \arrowtype{\T}{\tau} ~\mid~\arrowtype{\tau}{\T}$
460	\end{center}
461	Thus, if $\tau = \arrowtype{\tuple{P,T\ob{Q}}}{\Any}$, 
462	then $\tau = \T\context{P} = \T'\context{Q}$ where 
463	$\T = \arrowtype{\tuple{\emptycontext, T\ob{Q}}}{\Any}$ and 
464	$\T' = \arrowtype{\tuple{P, T\ob{\emptycontext}}}{\Any}$.
