536	Using type contexts, the last premise of rule \rncite{D-Method} ensures that
537	the class type parameters of a method appear in correct positions.
538	Considering the arrow type representing the method's type
539	$\tau' = \arrowtype{(\rep{\tau})}{\omega}$,
540	an invariant type parameter can appear anywhere in $\tau'$. However, 
541	when a covariant or contravariant type parameter $P$ appears in 
542	$\tau' = \T\context{P}$, the \textit{variance of the type context} of $P$
543	should match the variance $V$ of $P$: $\Delta\vdash{\T}\,\mathsf{variance}\,V$.
544	As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:contextvar},
545	the variance of a type context is defined inductively:
546	(1)~the hole is covariant;
547	(2)~components of tuple types, return types of arrow types, and
548	covariant type parameters of traits preserve
549	the variance of their enclosing contexts;
550	(3)~parameter types of arrow types and
551	contravariant type parameters of traits flip
552	the variance of their enclosing contexts; and
553	(4)~type parameters of objects and
554	invariant type parameters of traits are invariant.
555	For example, if $\tau = \arrowtype{\tuple{P,T\ob{Q}}}{\Any}$
556	is a method's type where $P$ and $Q$ are class type parameters and
557	$T$ has a contravariant type parameter,
558	then
559	$P$ is required to be contravariant or invariant and $Q$ is required to be covariant or invariant.

