102	The overloading rules require that every pair in a set of overloaded method
103	declarations satisfies all three rules defined in Figure~\ref{fig:overloadingrules}.
104	To determine whether one method declaration is more specific than the other,
105	while the informal rules in Section~\ref{sec:motiv:fgf} used the subset relation
106	of their applicable sets, which is not practically checkable,
107	we use the subtype relation of their domain types as in \citet{oopsla11}.
108	Note that even though FGFV additionally supports variance annotations of
109	trait type parameters, the overloading rules for FGF remain the same
110	because covariant or contravariant types affect only the well-formedness of
111	method declarations rather than the validity of overloading.
112	
113	If two method declarations have different names, they vacuously
114	satisfy all the rules ({\sc [No-Dup-Triv]}, {\sc [Meet-Triv]}, and
115	{\sc [Return-Triv]}).  The No Duplicates Rule also checks whether the
116	domain types of two declarations are different; if the domain type of one
117	declaration is not a subtype of the domain type of the other, they
118	satisfy the rule ({\sc [No-Dup-Not-Less]} and {\sc [No-Dup-Not-Gtr]}).
119	The Meet Rule is satisfied if two domain types exclude
120	each other ([{\sc Meet-Excl}]), they are in a subtype relation
121	({\sc [Meet-Less]} and {\sc [Meet-Gtr]}), or the domain type of a third
122	declaration of the same name is the intersection type of the domain types
123	of two declarations ({\sc [Meet-Third]}).  The Return Type Rule is
124	satisfied if two domain types are not in a subtype relation
125	({\sc [Return-Not-Less]}), or if two arrow types derived from the two declarations are
126	in a subtype relation ({\sc [Return-Test]}).
