234	\subsubsection{Dynamic Semantics}\label{sec:dynamic:sem}~\par
235	Dynamic overloading resolution uses additional metavariables as
236	in Figure~\ref{fig:dynamic}.  A class-declaration environment $\Psi$ is
237	a set of class declarations.  A ground type $g$ is a dynamically
238	available type, which is an internal type that does not contain type
239	parameters nor $\Bottom$; our type system requires that both static and
240	dynamic type inference algorithms instantiate type parameters with
241	non-$\Bottom$ types, and that all the type parameters are instantiated with
242	ground types at run time.  We call the run-time type of
243	a value the \textit{ilk} of the value; the ilk of an object instance
244	$O\obb{g}\tupleb{v}$ is $O\obb{g}$, and the ilk of a function
245	$((\bar{x \COLON g}) \COLON g' \Rightarrow \epsilon)$ is
246	$\tuple{\barearrowtype{(\bar{g})}{g'}}$.  Similarly to domain types,
247	we write $\tupleb{g}$ to denote ilks of multiple function parameters.
248	Evaluation contexts $E$ and redexes $R$ are conventional.
249	
250	For a method invocation, rule \rncite{R-Method} seeks the most
251	specific method declaration with a \textit{dispatchable instance} that
252	is applicable to the ilk of the arguments and whose return type is a
253	subtype of the annotated static return type.
254	We say a method declaration is \textit{dispatchable} to the ilk of
255	arguments and the static return type if it has a dispatchable instance.
256	For the sake of efficiency, rule \rncite{R-Method} first topologically sorts
257	all the visible method declarations in a most-to-least specific order.
258	% Since the specificity of method declarations is statically determinable,
259	% we can also topologically sort the method declarations at compile time
260	% to make dynamic overloading resolution more efficient.
261	(This order is statically determinable, so the sorting can be done at compile time.)
262	% When the sorted list $\listofb{d}$ is given, 
263	% the rule then checks the \textit{dispatch semipredicate} 
264	% for each method declaration from the beginning of the list 
265	The rule then checks the \textit{dispatch semipredicate} 
266	for each method declaration in the sorted list 
267	until it finds the first method declaration $d''$ dispatchable to the given
268	ilk of the arguments $k$ and the static return type $g$ with a dispatchable
269	instance $D=\instantiate{d''}{\sigma}$.
270	
271	
272	%\subsubsection{Dispatch Semipredicate}\label{sec:dispatch}~\par
273	The dispatch semipredicate defined in
274	% Figure~\ref{fig:dispatch}
275	rule \rncite{Dispatch}
276	checks if
277	a given method declaration $d$ is dispatchable to a given ilk $k$ and
278	a static return type $g$.  The dispatch semipredicate is satisfied using
279	substitution $\sigma$ if $d$ has a dispatchable instance (obtained by deleting the method type parameters of $d$
280	and applying $\sigma$ to its argument types, return type, and body).
281	Metavariable $\lbound$ and $a$ range over lower type bounds, and
282	$\ubound$ and $b$ range over upper type bounds.
283	We write $\listofb{\lbound}$ to denote ordered lists.
284	The dispatch semipredicate consists of three steps: (1)~computing type bounds
285	$\biglistofb{\listofb{\lbound}}$ and $\biglistofb{\listofb{\ubound}}$
286	that will make the domain type of 
287	the method declaration $d$ be a supertype of the ilk $k$, so that $d$ will be applicable to $k$,
288	(2)~computing type bounds
289	$\biglistofb{\listofb{\lbound'}}$ and $\biglistofb{\listofb{\ubound'}}$
290	that make the static return type $g$ a supertype of the return type of $d$
291	to make sure that the dynamic dispatch preserves type,
292	and (3)~solving those type bounds to get a valid substitution $\sigma$.
293	(If any of these steps fails, the semipredicate is false: the declaration is not applicable.)
294	The computed type bounds
295	% for the method type parameters
296	are either
297	ground types or $\Bottom$.  The dispatch semipredicate computes type
298	bounds by type matching as described in the next section.
