225	
226	The overloading rules of FGF require every pair in a set of
227	overloaded method declarations to satisfy \emph{all} of the following
228	rules as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:overloading}:
229	\begin{description}
230	\item[\textbf{No Duplicates Rule:}]
231	Distinct method declarations
232	must have different applicable sets
233	(there must be some argument type $\alpha$ for which one method is applicable and the other is not).
234	
235	\item[\textbf{Meet Rule:}] Let $\mathcal{S}$ be the intersection of the applicable sets of the two method declarations;
236	if $\mathcal{S}$ is not empty, then the overloaded set must contain a method declaration
237	(not necessarily distinct from the two given method declarations) whose applicable set is precisely $\mathcal{S}$.
238	
239	\item[\textbf{Return Type Rule:}] If one method declaration $d_1$ is
240	more specific than $d_2$, for any non-$\Bottom$ type $\alpha$ in the
241	applicable set of $d_1$, the return type of an instance of $d_2$ that is
242	applicable to $\alpha$ must be a supertype of the return type of
243	some instance of $d_1$ that is applicable to $\alpha$.
244	\end{description}
245	
246	Let us revisit the above ambiguous $\mbox{\textit{sort}}$ example
247	with an additional method declaration:
248	\[\begin{array}[ht]{r@{\qquad}l}
249	d_1:&\mbox{\textit{sort}}\ob{P \extends A}(x \COLON \kwd{List^I}\ob{P}) \COLON \kwd{SortedList^I}\ob{P} = \cdots\\
250	d_2:&\mbox{\textit{sort}}\ob{P \extends B}(x \COLON \kwd{List^I}\ob{P}) \COLON \kwd{SortedList^I}\ob{P} = \cdots\\
251	d_3:&\mbox{\textit{sort}}\ob{P \extends C}(x \COLON \kwd{List^I}\ob{P}) \COLON \kwd{SortedList^I}\ob{P} = \cdots\\
252	\end{array}\]
253	% This set of overloaded method declarations is valid since every pair
254	% satisfies all of the above overloading rules.
255	This set of overloaded method declarations is valid.
256	For all pairs, the No Duplicates Rule is trivially satisfied.
257	In the case of $d_1$ and
258	$d_2$, the Meet Rule is satisfied
259	% by $d_3$ being the meet of this pair,
260	($d_3$ is their meet),
261	and the Return Type Rule is vacuously satisfied since neither is more specific than the other.
262	% In the case of
263	For
264	$d_1$ and $d_3$, the Meet Rule is satisfied with $d_3$ itself as the meet; to see that
265	the Return Type Rule is satisfied, note that $\kwd{List^I}$ is an invariant
266	type constructor since FGF does not support variance.  For any
267	$\kwd{List^I}\ob{X}$ in the applicable set of $d_3$, there is exactly one instance
268	of $d_1$ (with $X$ substituted for $P$) applicable to it:
269	\begin{center}
270	$\mbox{\textit{sort}}(x \COLON \kwd{List^I}\ob{X}) \COLON \kwd{SortedList^I}\ob{X} = \cdots$.
271	\end{center}
272	Since the return type of this instance equals the return type of the corresponding instance of $d_3$: 
273	\begin{center}
274	$\mbox{\textit{sort}}(x \COLON \kwd{List^I}\ob{X}) \COLON \kwd{SortedList^I}\ob{X} = \cdots$,
275	\end{center}
276	$d_1$ and $d_3$ satisfy the Return Type Rule.  Similarly, $d_2$ and $d_3$
277	also satisfy all of the overloading rules.
278	
279	Again, the overloading rules ensure that there is no ambiguous method
280	calls at run time, and that types are preserved.  In FGF, No Duplicates
281	Rule rules out the trivial ambiguous calls with duplicate declarations.
282	Meet Rule plays an important role for ensuring the existence of a
283	disambiguating method declaration, and Return Type Rule serves a key
284	role for type preservation.
