287	\subsection{Overloading Rules for Multiple Inheritance and Generics with Variance}
288	\label{sec:motiv:fgfv}
289	Variance, as well as symmetric multiple dispatch, multiple inheritance,
290	and generics, may cause ambiguous method calls that cannot be resolved
291	at run time.  We revisit the ambiguous $\mbox{\textit{sort}}$ example in
292	Section~\ref{sec:motiv:fgf}.  This time, we define the same method
293	in a simpler way using covariant definitions of $\kwd{List^C}$ and $\kwd{SortedList^C}$.
294	Consider the following overloaded method declarations of $\mbox{\textit{sort}}$:
295	\[\begin{array}[ht]{r@{\qquad}l}
296	d_1:&\mbox{\textit{sort}}(x \COLON \kwd{List^C}\ob{A}) \COLON \kwd{SortedList^C}\ob{A} = \cdots\\
297	d_2:&\mbox{\textit{sort}}(x \COLON \kwd{List^C}\ob{B}) \COLON \kwd{SortedList^C}\ob{B} = \cdots\\
298	\end{array}\]
299	Similarly to the example in Section~\ref{sec:motiv:fgf}, for a method invocation
300	$\mbox{\textit{sort}}(l)$ where the run-time type of $l$ is $\kwd{List^C}\ob{C}$,
301	it is ambiguous which declaration to invoke: both $d_1$ and
302	$d_2$ are applicable to $l$ and neither is more specific than the other.
303	In order to ensure that there is no ambiguous method calls for
304	$\mbox{\textit{sort}}$ at run time, we need to add a disambiguating method
305	declaration $d_3$:
306	\[\begin{array}[ht]{r@{\qquad}l}
307	d_3:&\mbox{\textit{sort}}(x \COLON \kwd{List^C}\ob{C}) \COLON \kwd{SortedList^C}\ob{C} = \cdots\\
308	\end{array}\]
309	with a more specific return type than those of $d_1$ and $d_2$ for type
310	preservation.
311	% Thus, overloading rules should consider generic types with variance as well to avoid any unambiguous method calls at run time.
312	
313	Variance also adds complications in type-sound dynamic dispatch.  Let us now
314	assume that $\kwd{List^C}$ is covariant in its type argument but
315	$\kwd{SortedList^I}$ is invariant.
316	Then, the set of three method declarations above
317	replacing $\kwd{SortedList^C}$ with $\kwd{SortedList^I}$ would be invalid,
318	because the return type $\kwd{SortedList^I}\ob{A}$ of $d_1$ would not be a supertype of the return type $\kwd{SortedList^I}\ob{C}$ of $d_3$.
319	
320	Supporting variance in a type-sound manner requires keeping
321	track of the static types of method invocations at run time.
322	Let us again assume that $\kwd{List^C}$ is covariant but $\kwd{SortedList^I}$ is invariant,
323	and consider the following overloaded set (note that $d_3$ now has a type parameter $P$):
324	\[\begin{array}[ht]{r@{\qquad}r}
325	d_1:&\mbox{\textit{sort}}(x \COLON \kwd{List^C}\ob{A}) \COLON \kwd{SortedList^I}\ob{A} = \cdots\\
326	d_2:&\mbox{\textit{sort}}(x \COLON \kwd{List^C}\ob{B}) \COLON \kwd{SortedList^I}\ob{B} = \cdots\\
327	d_3:&\mbox{\textit{sort}}\ob{P}(x \COLON \kwd{List^C}\ob{C}) \COLON \kwd{SortedList^I}\ob{P} = \cdots\\
328	\end{array}\]
329	as well as the following generic method declaration of $\mbox{\textit{merge}}$:
330	\begin{center}
331	$\mbox{\textit{merge}}\ob{P}(x \COLON \kwd{SortedList^I}\ob{P}, y \COLON \kwd{SortedList^I}\ob{P}) \COLON \kwd{SortedList^I}\ob{P} = \cdots$
332	\end{center}
333	For a method invocation
334	$\mbox{\textit{merge}}(\mbox{\textit{sort}}(l_1), \mbox{\textit{sort}}(l_2))$
335	where the static types of $l_1$ and $l_2$ are both $\kwd{List^C}\ob{A}$ and
336	their run-time types are $\kwd{List^C}\ob{A}$ and $\kwd{List^C}\ob{C}$, respectively,
337	at compile time, both method invocations $\mbox{\textit{sort}}(l_1)$ and
338	$\mbox{\textit{sort}}(l_2)$ choose $d_1$ and their static types are both
339	$\kwd{SortedList^I}\ob{A}$.  Thus, the above $\mbox{\textit{merge}}$ call
340	statically invokes the following instance of the generic
341	$\mbox{\textit{merge}}$ declaration:
342	\begin{center}
343	$\mbox{\textit{merge}}(x \COLON \kwd{SortedList^I}\ob{A}, y \COLON \kwd{SortedList^I}\ob{A}) \COLON \kwd{SortedList^I}\ob{A}$
344	\end{center}
345	that is applicable to $\tuple{\kwd{SortedList^I}\ob{A}, \kwd{SortedList^I}\ob{A}}$.
346	However, at run time, while $\mbox{\textit{sort}}(l_1)$ chooses $d_1$ and
347	its return type is $\kwd{SortedList^I}\ob{A}$, $\mbox{\textit{sort}}(l_2)$
348	chooses $d_3$ and its return type depends on what actual type is inferred at run time for the type parameter $P$.
349	If the dynamic type inference algorithm instantiates $P$ as $A$,
350	$\mbox{\textit{sort}}(l_2)$ has type $\kwd{SortedList^I}\ob{A}$, which is
351	type sound.  But it could equally plausibly instantiate $P$ as some other type $T$,
352	and then no dynamic instance of the generic $\mbox{\textit{merge}}$
353	declaration would be applicable to 
354	$\tuple{\kwd{SortedList^I}\ob{A}, \kwd{SortedList^I}\ob{T}}$.
355	Hence, the $\mbox{\textit{merge}}$ call would fail, despite the fact that
356	the program successfully passed static type checking.
357	In order to help run-time type inference
358	algorithms make type-sound choices of type arguments, the language
359	semantics needs to make the static types of method invocations available at run time.
360	
361	In this paper, we present FGFV, which sheds light on the long-standing
362	endeavor to support strong types, symmetric multiple dispatch, multiple inheritance,
363	and parametric polymorphism with variance.
364	% Unlike existing approaches, FGFV captures only the core language features
365	% to address the problem, we formally define the FGFV calculus from its
366	% syntax, static semantics, and dynamic semantics, and we finally prove its
367	% type soundness.
368	We formalize the static overloading rules for FGFV using
369	existential types and universal types quantified over method type
370	parameters, and design a new dynamic dispatch algorithm that is proven
371	type-sound to guarantee unambiguous method calls and type preservation
372	at run time.
